summary: A smooth function on a manifold is minimized by steps that each minimize a quadratic model of it in the chart at the current point; Newton's model has the function's own curvature and converges quadratically, any other curvature with the exact gradient converges to the same points at a linear rate, and a trust region keeps the step where the model can be believed, judging it by the ratio of the decrease obtained to the decrease promised.
depends_on: [derivatives-in-charts, product-manifold]
---

<script>
	import Statement from '$lib/components/Statement.svelte';
	import Proof from '$lib/components/Proof.svelte';
	import * as newton from './statements/newton.md';
	import * as curvatureIteration from './statements/curvature-iteration.md';
	import * as trustRegionStep from './statements/trust-region-step.md';
</script>

A [mode](../map-estimation/) is the minimizer of a smooth function $f$, and except for the
simplest models it has no closed form. It is found by iteration: at the current point, replace $f$
by a model simple enough to minimize, minimize the model, and move. This note is about the model
every method of this part uses, a quadratic in the chart at the point, and about the two questions
any method built on it must answer: which curvature to give the model, and how far to believe it.
The answers to the first decide how fast an iteration converges, never where. The answer to the
second, the trust region, is what makes an iteration converge from a poor start at all.

Throughout, $f$ is a smooth function on a [product](../product-manifold/) of manifolds with
retractions, with the stacked tangent $\R^\dimension$, $\hat \point$ is the current point, and $g$
and $H$ are the [chart gradient and chart Hessian](../derivatives-in-charts/) of $f$ there. Every
result that compares charts at nearby points assumes that $\oplus$ is smooth near them.

## The quadratic model

Along a step $\tangent$ in the chart at $\hat \point$,

$$
	f(\hat \point \oplus \tangent) \approx m(\tangent) = f(\hat \point) + g\transpose \tangent + \tfrac12 \tangent\transpose M \tangent , @tag(model)
$$

for a symmetric matrix $M$, the model's *curvature*. The model agrees with $f$ in value and
gradient at $\tangent = 0$ whatever $M$ is. With $M$ the Hessian of the pull-back $\tangent \mapsto
f(\hat \point \oplus \tangent)$ it agrees to second order as well, and that Hessian equals the chart
Hessian at a stationary point by [%full](statement:math/derivatives-in-charts/chart-hessian). When
$M$ is positive definite the model has one minimizer, the solution of

$$
	M \tangent = -g , @tag(step)
$$

and the iteration moves to $\hat \point \oplus \tangent$. The step is a *descent direction*,
$g\transpose \tangent = -g\transpose M^{-1} g < 0$ whenever $g \ne 0$, so a short enough step along
it decreases $f$. When $M$ is indefinite, the model has no minimizer, and [](eq:step) solves for a
stationary point of the model that may be a saddle or a maximum.

## Newton's method

Newton's method takes the model's curvature from $f$: $M = H$, solved as [](eq:step) at every
iterate.

<Statement {...newton} />

<Proof>

Read the iteration in the chart at $q^\ast$, as the map $\Phi(q) = q \oplus \delta(q)$ with
$\delta(q) = -H(q)^{-1} g(q)$. It is smooth near $q^\ast$, since $H$ is continuous and invertible at
$q^\ast$, and $\Phi(q^\ast) = q^\ast$ since $g(q^\ast) = 0$. The derivative of $q \oplus \delta$ in
$q$ at $\delta = 0$ is the identity, and its derivative in $\delta$ at $\delta = 0$ is the identity
too, by the defining property of a retraction. The derivative of $\delta$ at $q^\ast$ is
$-H(q^\ast)^{-1}$ times the derivative of $g$ there, the term from the derivative of $H^{-1}$
multiplying $g(q^\ast) = 0$, and the derivative of $g$ along $\tangent$ is $H(q^\ast)\, \tangent$ by
the definition [](eq:math/derivatives-in-charts/chart-hessian). So

$$
	\D \Phi(q^\ast) = I - H(q^\ast)^{-1} H(q^\ast) = 0 .
$$

$\Phi$ is twice continuously differentiable near $q^\ast$, so by Taylor's theorem in the chart's
coordinates $\lvert \Phi(q) \ominus q^\ast \rvert \le C \lvert q \ominus q^\ast \rvert^2$ for $q$ in a
ball about $q^\ast$, with $C$ the bound of half the second derivative of $\Phi$ there. On the
smaller ball where $C \lvert q \ominus q^\ast \rvert < 1$ the map contracts, so an iterate that
starts there stays there and the bound holds at every step.

</Proof>

Quadratic convergence doubles the number of correct digits at every step, and is the best that any
method of this kind achieves. It comes at two costs. The first is the Hessian itself: second
derivatives of every term, which for a function of many variables is more work, and more code, than
the gradient. The second is that nothing in the proof asked for a minimum. Newton's method
converges as fast to a maximum or a saddle as to a minimum, and away from a minimum, where $H$ may
be indefinite, its step may increase $f$.

## Any curvature

A curvature other than the Hessian gives up the quadratic rate and keeps everything else.

<Statement {...curvatureIteration} />

<Proof>

$M(q_k)$ is invertible, so $\delta_k = 0$ exactly when $g(q_k) = 0$. Read the iteration in the chart
at $q^\ast$ as the map $\Phi(q) = q \oplus \bigl(-M(q)^{-1} g(q)\bigr)$. As in the proof of
[%full](statement:newton), $\D \Phi(q^\ast) = I - (M^\ast)^{-1} H^\ast$. A fixed point of a map whose
derivative there has spectral radius $\rho < 1$ attracts every start near enough to it, at the
linear rate $\rho$, by Ostrowski's theorem [](cite:ortega1970).

</Proof>

So the gradient decides where an iteration stops, and the curvature only how fast it gets there.
Any curvature that is invertible leaves the stationary points of $f$ where they were; Newton's
curvature makes $\rho = 0$; one that is wrong by a little costs a slow linear rate, and one that is
wrong by much, $\rho \ge 1$, costs convergence, without the iteration ever stopping at a point
that is not stationary. A curvature that is positive definite everywhere, as Newton's is not, makes
every step a descent direction. The methods of this part are each a choice of $M$: the
Gauss–Newton matrix of a sum of squares, which needs first derivatives alone and is positive
semidefinite by construction, and the expected information of a distribution, which is the same
matrix where it applies and differs from it where a scale is estimated.

## The trust region

Every model of this kind is good only near $\hat \point$. From a poor start the minimizer of the
model lies far from it, where $f$ is nothing like $m$, and the step overshoots. A *trust region*
limits the step to where the model is believed,

$$
	\min_{\tangent} m(\tangent) \quad \text{subject to} \quad \lvert \tangent \rvert_D \le \Delta , @tag(subproblem)
$$

for a *radius* $\Delta > 0$ and a norm $\lvert \tangent \rvert_D = (\tangent\transpose D
\tangent)^{1/2}$ with $D$ a positive diagonal matrix, which puts the coordinates on a common scale.
Its solution, the *trust-region step*, is characterized by a single scalar.

<Statement {...trustRegionStep} />

<Proof>

For part 1, $m_\lambda(\tangent) = m(\tangent) + \tfrac12 \lambda \lvert \tangent \rvert_D^2$ is
convex, since $M + \lambda D$ is positive semidefinite, and its gradient $(M + \lambda D)\,
\tangent + g$ vanishes at $\tangent^\ast$, so $\tangent^\ast$ minimizes it over all of
$\R^\dimension$. The condition $\lambda (\Delta - \lvert \tangent^\ast \rvert_D) = 0$ gives $\lambda
\lvert \tangent^\ast \rvert_D^2 = \lambda \Delta^2$. For $\lvert \tangent \rvert_D \le \Delta$, then,

$$
	\begin{aligned}
		m(\tangent) = m_\lambda(\tangent) - \tfrac12 \lambda \lvert \tangent \rvert_D^2
		&\ge m_\lambda(\tangent^\ast) - \tfrac12 \lambda \Delta^2 \\
		&= m(\tangent^\ast) .
	\end{aligned}
$$

For parts 2 and 3, write $u = D^{1/2} \tangent$, $\tilde M = D^{-1/2} M D^{-1/2} = V
\operatorname{diag}(\mu_i) V\transpose$ and $\tilde g = D^{-1/2} g$. For $\lambda$ with $\tilde M +
\lambda I$ invertible, the solution of $(M + \lambda D)\, \tangent = -g$ has

$$
	\lvert \tangent(\lambda) \rvert_D^2 = \sum_i \frac{(v_i\transpose \tilde g)^2}{(\mu_i + \lambda)^2} ,
$$

which does not increase with $\lambda > 0$, since every $\mu_i \ge 0$. That is the second half of
part 3, and the first is part 1 with $\Delta = \lvert \tangent(\lambda) \rvert_D$. For part 2, the
$\mu_i$ are positive. If $\lvert M^{-1} g \rvert_D \le \Delta$, $\lambda = 0$ and $\tangent^\ast =
-M^{-1} g$ satisfy part 1. Otherwise $g \ne 0$, the sum is continuous and strictly decreasing in
$\lambda \ge 0$, exceeds $\Delta^2$ at $\lambda = 0$ and tends to zero, so exactly one $\lambda > 0$
gives $\lvert \tangent(\lambda) \rvert_D = \Delta$. The minimizer is unique because $m$ is strictly
convex, and so is $\lambda$, which $(M + \lambda D)\, \tangent^\ast = -g$ determines when
$\tangent^\ast \ne 0$; when $\tangent^\ast = 0$, $\lambda = 0$ by the third condition.

</Proof>

The scalar $\lambda$ is a *damping*. At $\lambda = 0$ the step is the model's own minimizer, and as
$\lambda$ grows it shortens and turns toward $-D^{-1} g / \lambda$, a short step down the gradient
in the scale of $D$. Part 3 says that a method may choose $\lambda$ instead of $\Delta$: every
damped step is the trust-region step of some radius, and a larger damping a smaller radius. Doing
so saves solving for the $\lambda$ of a given radius, and changes nothing else. With a curvature
that is only positive semidefinite, or indefinite, as Newton's may be, the subproblem still has a
solution, characterized as in part 1 with $M + \lambda D$ required to be positive semidefinite,
and computing it needs more care where $g$ is orthogonal to the eigenvectors of the least
eigenvalue [](cite:more1983).

## The gain ratio

A trust-region method adapts its radius to what the steps teach it about the model. A step
$\tangent$ promises the decrease $m(0) - m(\tangent)$, which is positive for every solution of
[](eq:subproblem) when $g \ne 0$, and obtains $f(\hat \point) - f(\hat \point \oplus \tangent)$.
Their ratio,

$$
	\varrho = \frac{f(\hat \point) - f(\hat \point \oplus \tangent)}{m(0) - m(\tangent)} , @tag(gain)
$$

is the *gain ratio*. It is one when $f$ is the model over the step, near one where the model is
good, and small or negative where it is not. Every trust-region method uses it the same way:

- a step with $\varrho$ above a small threshold, often zero, is accepted, and the iteration moves
  to $\hat \point \oplus \tangent$; one below it is rejected, and the iteration stays;
- a step with $\varrho$ near one, which the model predicted well, lets the radius grow, or the
  damping shrink; a step with small $\varrho$ makes the radius shrink, or the damping grow.

The methods differ in the thresholds and the factors, and in whether they adapt $\Delta$ or
$\lambda$. With a curvature that is bounded, a function that is bounded below and an acceptance
threshold above zero, the gradients of such a method's iterates tend to zero from any start, and
near a minimum at which the model's curvature is the Hessian it takes the full step and inherits
Newton's rate [Chapter 4](cite:nocedal2006). The trust region is what turns a step that converges from near
the answer into a method that converges from far from it.
